  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA 500mb trough axis rotates across the Great Lakes tonight into\nThursday, before a transition to a quasi-zonal pattern tomorrow\nafternoon through Friday. A weak, moisture-starved cold front is\nslated to cross the Upper Ohio Valley late tonight into Thursday\nmorning. A mostly dry passage is figured, but a few sprinkles\ncannot be totally ruled out north of Pittsburgh tonight as the \nboundary may scoop up some minimal moisture from the relatively \nwarm Lake Erie waters.\n\nDry weather then continues through Friday and Saturday as\nsurface high pressure remains in control. Temperatures remain a\na bit above climatology through Friday. Southerly flow and\nrising heights behind the departing high may push daytime highs\nsome 10 degrees above climatology on Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nCurrent NHC guidance still has Isaias landfalling on the Gulf\nCoast Friday night, and then its remnant low reaching the Lower\nOhio Valley later Sunday night or early Monday morning. Model\nguidance starts to diverge thereafter on the exact track, but\nmost have the low turning east and nearing or crossing the \nAtlantic coast by Monday. This relatively speedy crossing over\nor near our region will most likely keep rainfall amounts from\nbecoming excessive. There does seem to be a lean towards a\nfaster arrival time of the rain, with onset possible around or\nafter midnight Saturday night.\n\nThe 13Z NBM has 48-hour mean rainfall totals through 8 AM \nMonday of between 0.8 and 1.2 inches, with a north-to-south\ngradient. 50th percentile levels are relatively similar to the\nmean values, suggesting less of a bimodal distribution in \nsolutions than from the NBM runs yesterday. However, there has\nbeen an increase in the mean values as compared to yesterday.\n90th percentile levels now fall in the range of 1.75 to 2.25\ninches, also higher than yesterday. This is still below current\n6-hour flash flood guidance values, which are above 2.5 inches\nareawide save for very small pockets. Overall, the threat of\nwidespread flooding concerns remains low, although isolated \nurban and poor drainage issues could in theory arise. In \nparticular, localized street flooding from debris-blocked storm \ndrains could be a problem in the higher-end rainfall scenarios. \nTrends will be monitored. If slower motion of the remnant low \nbecomes more likely, then higher rainfall totals could be more \nof a concern. Severe thunderstorms risks are also very low. The \nprobability of 250 J/kg of SBCAPE remains under 30%, and longer-\nrange machine learning guidance keeps severe probabilities less\nthan 5%.\n\nWith the faster overall motion, any heavy rainfall threat\ncurrently decreases after 12Z Monday as the remnants head to our\neast. Still, relatively light showers may linger into Tuesday as\nsome long-range scenarios bring another shortwave across the\nGreat Lakes.",
